<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">Decentralized Stochastic Nonconvex Optimization 
<br class="ltx_break">under the Relaxed Smoothness</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Luo Luo
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Xue Cui
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Tingkai Jia
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Cheng Chen
</span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">This paper studies decentralized optimization problem <math id="abstract1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(\mathbf{x})=\frac{1}{m}\sum_{i=1}^{m}f_{i}(\mathbf{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>m</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(\mathbf{x})=\frac{1}{m}\sum_{i=1}^{m}f_{i}(\mathbf{x})</annotation></semantics></math>,
where each local function has the form of <math id="abstract1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i}(\mathbf{x})={\mathbb{E}}\left[F(\mathbf{x};{\bm{\xi}}_{i})\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝝃</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i}(\mathbf{x})={\mathbb{E}}\left[F(\mathbf{x};{\bm{\xi}}_{i})\right]</annotation></semantics></math> which is <math id="abstract1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(L_{0},L_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(L_{0},L_{1})</annotation></semantics></math>-smooth but possibly nonconvex and the random variable <math id="abstract1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{\xi}}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝝃</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{\xi}}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> follows distribution <math id="abstract1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{D}}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{D}}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We propose a novel algorithm called decentralized normalized stochastic gradient descent (DNSGD), which can achieve the <math id="abstract1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-stationary point on each local agent.
We present a new framework for analyzing decentralized first-order methods in the relaxed smooth setting, based on the Lyapunov function related to the product of the gradient norm and the consensus error.
The analysis shows upper bounds on sample complexity of <math id="abstract1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{O}}(m^{-1}(L_{f}\sigma^{2}\Delta_{f}\epsilon^{-4}+\sigma^{2}\epsilon^{-2}+L_{f}^{-2}L_{1}^{3}\sigma^{2}\Delta_{f}\epsilon^{-1}+L_{f}^{-2}L_{1}^{2}\sigma^{2}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>m</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{O}}(m^{-1}(L_{f}\sigma^{2}\Delta_{f}\epsilon^{-4}+\sigma^{2}\epsilon^{-2}+L_{f}^{-2}L_{1}^{3}\sigma^{2}\Delta_{f}\epsilon^{-1}+L_{f}^{-2}L_{1}^{2}\sigma^{2}))</annotation></semantics></math> per agent
and communication complexity of <math id="abstract1.m8" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\tilde{\mathcal{O}}((L_{f}\epsilon^{-2}+L_{1}\epsilon^{-1})\gamma^{-1/2}\Delta_{f})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msup><mi>γ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathcal{O}}((L_{f}\epsilon^{-2}+L_{1}\epsilon^{-1})\gamma^{-1/2}\Delta_{f})</annotation></semantics></math>,
where <math id="abstract1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{f}=L_{0}+L_{1}\zeta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ζ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{f}=L_{0}+L_{1}\zeta</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="abstract1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is the variance of the stochastic gradient, <math id="abstract1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{f}</annotation></semantics></math> is the initial optimal function value gap, <math id="abstract1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> is the spectral gap of the network, and <math id="abstract1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\zeta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ζ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\zeta</annotation></semantics></math> is the degree of the gradient dissimilarity.
In the special case of <math id="abstract1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{1}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{1}=0</annotation></semantics></math>, the above results (nearly) match the lower bounds on decentralized nonconvex optimization in the standard smooth setting.
We also conduct numerical experiments to show the empirical superiority of our method.</p>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This paper considers the decentralized stochastic optimization over a connected network of <math id="S1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> nodes. Specifically, we study the minimization problem</p>
<table id="S1.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S1.Ex1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\min_{{\bf{x}}\in{\mathbb{R}}^{d}}f({\bf{x}})=\frac{1}{m}\sum_{i=1}^{m}f_{i}({\bf{x}})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>m</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\min_{{\bf{x}}\in{\mathbb{R}}^{d}}f({\bf{x}})=\frac{1}{m}\sum_{i=1}^{m}f_{i}({\bf{x}}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i}:{\mathbb{R}}^{d}\to{\mathbb{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i}:{\mathbb{R}}^{d}\to{\mathbb{R}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the local function on <math id="S1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>th agent and has the form of</p>
<table id="S1.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S1.Ex2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle f_{i}({\bf{x}})={\mathbb{E}}\left[F_{i}({\bf{x}};{\bm{\xi}}_{i})\right]." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝝃</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle f_{i}({\bf{x}})={\mathbb{E}}\left[F_{i}({\bf{x}};{\bm{\xi}}_{i})\right].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">We are interested in the setting that each <math id="S1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is relaxed smooth <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">54</a>]</cite> but possibly nonconvex and the the random variable <math id="S1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{\xi}}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝝃</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{\xi}}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> follows distribution <math id="S1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{D}}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{D}}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The optimization theory and algorithms for relaxed smooth problem have received significant attention in recent years <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>, <a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">46</a>, <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>, <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>, <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>, <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>, <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, <a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">44</a>, <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>, <a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">45</a>, <a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>, <a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">48</a>, <a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">53</a>, <a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">51</a>, <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>, <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>, <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>,
since empirical studies suggest the relaxed smoothness can better characterize the training of neural network models than classical smoothness <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">54</a>, <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>, <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>.
However, most existing works focus on centralized scenarios, and how to well address the relaxed smooth optimization problem in decentralized scenarios remains unclear.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The main challenge in decentralized optimization is that each agent can only access the information from itself or its neighbors, which leads to the consensus error among local variables <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>]</cite>.
For smooth optimization, the gradient tracking technique successfully uses the gradient Lipschitz continuity to reduce the consensus error and guarantee the convergence of the algorithm <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>, <a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>]</cite>.
Additionally, the multi-consensus steps with Chebyshev acceleration <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>, <a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite> can further improve the communication complexity, leading to the optimal decentralized methods for both convex and nonconvex cases <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>, <a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">49</a>, <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>, <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>, <a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>, <a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>, <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>, <a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">50</a>, <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>, <a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">52</a>, <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite>.
For nonsmooth optimization, the recent works focus on finding the (generalized) stationary points of the Lipschitz continuous objective <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>, <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>, <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>, <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite>, establishing efficient decentralized stochastic algorithms based on the boundedness of local (sub)gradients.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper, we focus on decentralized optimization under the relaxed smoothness, which is more challenging than above classical settings since neither the local functions nor their gradients enjoy the Lipschitz continuity.
We propose a novel algorithm called decentralized normalized stochastic gradient descent (DNSGD) to address above issues.
Our method does not include the gradient clipping, avoiding the potential inconsistency in the update modes of local variable arising from consensus errors.
We further construct a new Lyapunov function related to the product
of the gradient norm and the consensus error, which successfully characterizes the
convergence of our decentralized method in the relaxed smooth setting that allows local gradients to be neither bounded nor Lipschitz continuous.
Moreover, we conduct empirical studies to show the empirical advantage of our proposed method in practice.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Preliminaries</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We use the bold lowercase letter with the subscript to represent the local variable, e.g., vector <math id="S2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf{x}}_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf{x}}_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> is a local variable on the <math id="S2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>th agent.
We use the bold uppercase letter to present the aggregated matrix consisting of local variables from all agents, e.g.,
<math id="S2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}=[\mathbf{x}_{1},\dots,\mathbf{x}_{m}]^{\top}\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}=[\mathbf{x}_{1},\dots,\mathbf{x}_{m}]^{\top}\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times d}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf{x}}_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf{x}}_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We also denote the mean vector by the bold lowercase letter with bar, e.g., <math id="S2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{{\bf{x}}}=\frac{1}{m}{\bf{1}}^{\top}{\bf X}\in{\mathbb{R}}^{1\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>m</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mn>𝟏</mn><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐗</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{{\bf{x}}}=\frac{1}{m}{\bf{1}}^{\top}{\bf X}\in{\mathbb{R}}^{1\times d}</annotation></semantics></math>,
where <math id="S2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf{1}}=[1,\dots,1]\in{\mathbb{R}}^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>𝟏</mn><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>m</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf{1}}=[1,\dots,1]\in{\mathbb{R}}^{m}</annotation></semantics></math>.
In addition, the notation <math id="S2.p1.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\|\cdot\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∥</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em">∥</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\cdot\|</annotation></semantics></math> represents the Euclidean norm (or the Frobenius) norm for given vector (or matrix).
In addition, we use <math id="S2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐈</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf I}</annotation></semantics></math> to present the identity matrix.
For the ease of presentation, we allow the input of the function be organized as both column vector or row vector.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We impose following common assumptions for nonconvex optimization under the relaxed smoothness.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmasm1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_asm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmasm1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Assumption 1</span></span><span id="Thmasm1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(lower boundedness)<span id="Thmasm1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmasm1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmasm1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmasm1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">We suppose the objective <math id="Thmasm1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is lower bounded, i.e., there exists some <math id="Thmasm1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{*}\in{\mathbb{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}\in{\mathbb{R}}</annotation></semantics></math> such that</span></p>
<table id="S2.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle f^{*}=\inf_{{\bf{x}}\in{\mathbb{R}}^{d}}f({\bf{x}})&gt;-\infty." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">inf</mo><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></munder><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle f^{*}=\inf_{{\bf{x}}\in{\mathbb{R}}^{d}}f({\bf{x}})&gt;-\infty.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmasm2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_asm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmasm2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Assumption 2</span></span><span id="Thmasm2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(relaxed smoothness)<span id="Thmasm2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmasm2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmasm2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmasm2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">We suppose each local function <math id="Thmasm2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="Thmasm2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(L_{0},L_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(L_{0},L_{1})</annotation></semantics></math>-smooth i.e., there exists some <math id="Thmasm2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{0},L_{1}\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{0},L_{1}\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> such that each local function <math id="Thmasm2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies</span></p>
<table id="S2.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\left\|\nabla f_{i}({\bf{x}})-\nabla f_{i}({\bf{y}})\right\|\leq\left(L_{0}+L_{1}\left\|\nabla f_{i}({\bf{x}})\right\|\right)\left\|{\bf{x}}-{\bf{y}}\right\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>𝐲</mi></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\left\|\nabla f_{i}({\bf{x}})-\nabla f_{i}({\bf{y}})\right\|\leq\left(L_{0}+L_{1}\left\|\nabla f_{i}({\bf{x}})\right\|\right)\left\|{\bf{x}}-{\bf{y}}\right\|</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmasm2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmasm2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">for all <math id="Thmasm2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf{x}},{\bf{y}}\in{\mathbb{R}}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐲</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf{x}},{\bf{y}}\in{\mathbb{R}}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="Thmasm2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\|{\bf{x}}-{\bf{y}}\right\|\leq 1/L_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>𝐲</mi></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\|{\bf{x}}-{\bf{y}}\right\|\leq 1/L_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, where we define <math id="Thmasm2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/L_{1}=+\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/L_{1}=+\infty</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="Thmasm2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{1}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{1}=0</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmremark1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmremark1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 1</span></span><span id="Thmremark1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmremark1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmremark1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmremark1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The notion of relaxed smoothness was first introduced by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al. [54]</a></cite>, which requires the second-order differentiability.
Here, we consider the weaker condition (Assumption <a href="#Thmasm2" title="Assumption 2 (relaxed smoothness). ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Decentralized Stochastic Nonconvex Optimization under the Relaxed Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) characterized by the gradient, which is provided by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al. [53]</a></cite>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We also introduce the assumption on gradient dissimilarity to describes the heterogeneity of the local functions, which is widely used in distributed optimization <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">47</a>, <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmasm3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_asm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmasm3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Assumption 3</span></span><span id="Thmasm3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(bounded gradient dissimilarity)<span id="Thmasm3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmasm3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmasm3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmasm3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">We suppose there exists some <math id="Thmasm3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\zeta\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ζ</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\zeta\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> such that the local gradient satisfies</span></p>
<table id="S2.EGx5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\left\|\nabla f_{i}({\bf{x}})-\nabla f({\bf{x}})\right\|\leq\zeta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>ζ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\left\|\nabla f_{i}({\bf{x}})-\nabla f({\bf{x}})\right\|\leq\zeta</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmasm3.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmasm3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">for all <math id="Thmasm3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in[m]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in[m]</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmasm3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf{x}}\in{\mathbb{R}}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf{x}}\in{\mathbb{R}}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The following proposition shows that the objective <math id="S2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is relaxed smooth if the local functions are relaxed smooth with bounded dissimilarity.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmprop1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmprop1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 1</span></span><span id="Thmprop1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmprop1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmprop1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmprop1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Under Assumptions <a href="#Thmasm2" title="Assumption 2 (relaxed smoothness). ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Decentralized Stochastic Nonconvex Optimization under the Relaxed Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> and <a href="#Thmasm3" title="Assumption 3 (bounded gradient dissimilarity). ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Decentralized Stochastic Nonconvex Optimization under the Relaxed Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, the objective function <math id="Thmprop1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f({\bf{x}})=\frac{1}{m}\sum_{i=1}^{m}f_{i}({\bf{x}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>m</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f({\bf{x}})=\frac{1}{m}\sum_{i=1}^{m}f_{i}({\bf{x}})</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="Thmprop1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(L_{f},L_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(L_{f},L_{1})</annotation></semantics></math>-smooth with <math id="Thmprop1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{f}=L_{0}+L_{1}\zeta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ζ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{f}=L_{0}+L_{1}\zeta</annotation></semantics></math>,
i.e., we have</span></p>
<table id="S2.EGx6" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\left\|\nabla f({\bf{x}})-\nabla f({\bf{y}})\right\|\leq\left(L_{f}+L_{1}\left\|\nabla f({\bf{x}})\right\|\right)\left\|{\bf{x}}-{\bf{y}}\right\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>𝐲</mi></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\left\|\nabla f({\bf{x}})-\nabla f({\bf{y}})\right\|\leq\left(L_{f}+L_{1}\left\|\nabla f({\bf{x}})\right\|\right)\left\|{\bf{x}}-{\bf{y}}\right\|</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmprop1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmprop1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">for all <math id="Thmprop1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf{x}},{\bf{y}}\in{\mathbb{R}}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐲</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf{x}},{\bf{y}}\in{\mathbb{R}}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="Thmprop1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\|{\bf{x}}-{\bf{y}}\right\|\leq 1/L_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>𝐲</mi></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\|{\bf{x}}-{\bf{y}}\right\|\leq 1/L_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We are interested in decentralize stochastic first-order methods, which can access the stochastic first-order oracle <math id="S2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla F({\bf{x}};{\bm{\xi}}_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝝃</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla F({\bf{x}};{\bm{\xi}}_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> that satisfies the following assumption.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmasm4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_asm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmasm4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Assumption 4</span></span><span id="Thmasm4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmasm4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmasm4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmasm4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For given <math id="Thmasm4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in[m]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in[m]</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmasm4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf{x}}\in{\mathbb{R}}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf{x}}\in{\mathbb{R}}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, we suppose the <math id="Thmasm4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>th agent can draw <math id="Thmasm4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{\xi}}_{i}\sim{\mathcal{D}}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝛏</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{\xi}}_{i}\sim{\mathcal{D}}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and access the stochastic first-order oracle <math id="Thmasm4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla F_{i}({\bf{x}};{\bm{\xi}}_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝛏</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla F_{i}({\bf{x}};{\bm{\xi}}_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> which satisfies</span></p>
<table id="S2.EGx7" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle{\mathbb{E}}[\nabla F_{i}({\bf{x}};{\bm{\xi}}_{i})]=\nabla f_{i}({\bf{x}})\qquad\text{and}\qquad{\mathbb{E}}\left[\left\|\nabla F_{i}({\bf{x}};{\bm{\xi}}_{i})-\nabla f_{i}({\bf{x}})\right\|^{2}\right]\leq\sigma^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝝃</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">and</mtext><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝝃</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle{\mathbb{E}}[\nabla F_{i}({\bf{x}};{\bm{\xi}}_{i})]=\nabla f_{i}({\bf{x}})\qquad\text{and}\qquad{\mathbb{E}}\left[\left\|\nabla F_{i}({\bf{x}};{\bm{\xi}}_{i})-\nabla f_{i}({\bf{x}})\right\|^{2}\right]\leq\sigma^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmasm4.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmasm4.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">for some <math id="Thmasm4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">In decentralized setting, each agent can only exchange information with its neighbors in per communication round.
One communication step in decentralized optimization is typically presented by multiplication on the mixing matrix <math id="S2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf W}\in{\mathbb{R}}^{m\times m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐖</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf W}\in{\mathbb{R}}^{m\times m}</annotation></semantics></math>, which satisfies the following standard assumption <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>, <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>, <a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">50</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmasm5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_asm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmasm5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Assumption 5</span></span><span id="Thmasm5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmasm5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmasm5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmasm5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmasm5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf W}\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐖</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf W}\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times m}</annotation></semantics></math> be the mixing matrix associated with the connected and undirected network.
We suppose
(a) the matrix <math id="Thmasm5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf W}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐖</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf W}</annotation></semantics></math> is symmetric with <math id="Thmasm5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{i,j}\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{i,j}\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="Thmasm5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i,j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i,j</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="Thmasm5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{i,j}\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{i,j}\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if the <math id="Thmasm5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>th agent and the <math id="Thmasm5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>th agent are connected or <math id="Thmasm5.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=j</annotation></semantics></math>;
(b) It holds <math id="Thmasm5.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{0}\preceq{\bf W}\preceq{\bf I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>𝟎</mn><mo>⪯</mo><mi>𝐖</mi><mo>⪯</mo><mi>𝐈</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{0}\preceq{\bf W}\preceq{\bf I}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmasm5.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf W}^{\top}\mathbf{1}={\bf W}\mathbf{1}=\mathbf{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝐖</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>𝟏</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>𝐖𝟏</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>𝟏</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf W}^{\top}\mathbf{1}={\bf W}\mathbf{1}=\mathbf{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="Thmasm5.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{null}({\bf I}-{\bf W})=\operatorname{span}(\mathbf{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>null</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝐈</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>𝐖</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>span</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟏</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{null}({\bf I}-{\bf W})=\operatorname{span}(\mathbf{1})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p7.1" class="ltx_p">We use <math id="S2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{2}({\bf W})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐖</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{2}({\bf W})</annotation></semantics></math> to present the second largest eigenvalue of the mixing matrix <math id="S2.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf W}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐖</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf W}</annotation></semantics></math>. Under Assumption <a href="#Thmasm5" title="Assumption 5. ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Decentralized Stochastic Nonconvex Optimization under the Relaxed Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, we have <math id="S2.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{2}({\bf W})\in[0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐖</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{2}({\bf W})\in[0,1)</annotation></semantics></math>, then we define the spectral gap of <math id="S2.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf W}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐖</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf W}</annotation></semantics></math> as</p>
<table id="S2.EGx8" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\gamma=1-\lambda_{2}({\bf W})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐖</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\gamma=1-\lambda_{2}({\bf W}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p7.2" class="ltx_p">Additionally, we present the multi-consensus steps with Chebyshev acceleration in Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Decentralized Stochastic Nonconvex Optimization under the Relaxed Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>, <a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>, which encourage all local variables to converge to the average as follows.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmprop2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmprop2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 2</span></span><span id="Thmprop2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ye et al. [50, Proposition 1]</a></cite>)<span id="Thmprop2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmprop2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmprop2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmprop2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Under Assumption <a href="#Thmasm5" title="Assumption 5. ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Decentralized Stochastic Nonconvex Optimization under the Relaxed Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, the output of Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Decentralized Stochastic Nonconvex Optimization under the Relaxed Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> holds</span></p>
<table id="S2.EGx9" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex7"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\frac{1}{m}\mathbf{1}^{\top}{\bf Y}^{(K)}=\bar{{\bf{y}}}^{(0)}\quad\text{and}\quad\big\|{\bf Y}^{(K)}-\mathbf{1}\bar{{\bf{y}}}^{(0)}\big\|\leq c_{1}\big(1-c_{2}\sqrt{1-\lambda_{2}({\bf W})}\,\big)^{K}\big\|{\bf Y}^{(0)}-\mathbf{1}\bar{{\bf{y}}}^{(0)}\big\|," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>m</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mn>𝟏</mn><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝐘</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">and</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">‖</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐘</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>𝟏</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">‖</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐖</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mi>K</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">‖</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐘</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>𝟏</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">‖</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\frac{1}{m}\mathbf{1}^{\top}{\bf Y}^{(K)}=\bar{{\bf{y}}}^{(0)}\quad\text{and}\quad\big\|{\bf Y}^{(K)}-\mathbf{1}\bar{{\bf{y}}}^{(0)}\big\|\leq c_{1}\big(1-c_{2}\sqrt{1-\lambda_{2}({\bf W})}\,\big)^{K}\big\|{\bf Y}^{(0)}-\mathbf{1}\bar{{\bf{y}}}^{(0)}\big\|,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmprop2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmprop2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="Thmprop2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{{\bf{y}}}^{(0)}=\frac{1}{m}\mathbf{1}^{\top}{\bf Y}^{(0)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>m</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mn>𝟏</mn><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝐘</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{{\bf{y}}}^{(0)}=\frac{1}{m}\mathbf{1}^{\top}{\bf Y}^{(0)}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmprop2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{1}=\sqrt{14}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mn>14</mn></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{1}=\sqrt{14}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="Thmprop2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{2}=1-1/\sqrt{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{2}=1-1/\sqrt{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<figure id="alg1" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 1</span> </span> <math id="alg1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm AccGossip}({\bf Y}^{(0)},{\bf W},K)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>AccGossip</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐘</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐖</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm AccGossip}({\bf Y}^{(0)},{\bf W},K)</annotation></semantics></math></figcaption>
<div id="alg1.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg1.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">1:</span></span>
            
            
           <math id="alg1.l1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf Y}^{(-1)}={\bf Y}^{(0)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐘</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>𝐘</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf Y}^{(-1)}={\bf Y}^{(0)}</annotation></semantics></math> 
</div>
<div id="alg1.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">2:</span></span>
            
            
           <math id="alg1.l2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{y}=(1-\sqrt{1-(\lambda_{2}({\bf W}))^{2}}\,)/(1+\sqrt{1-(\lambda_{2}({\bf W}))^{2}}\,)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐖</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msqrt></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐖</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msqrt></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{y}=(1-\sqrt{1-(\lambda_{2}({\bf W}))^{2}}\,)/(1+\sqrt{1-(\lambda_{2}({\bf W}))^{2}}\,)</annotation></semantics></math> 
</div>
<div id="alg1.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">3:</span></span>
            
            
           <span id="alg1.l3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> <math id="alg1.l3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=0,1,\dots,K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=0,1,\dots,K</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l4" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">4:</span></span>
            
            
            <math id="alg1.l4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf Y}^{(k+1)}=(1+\eta_{y}){\bf W}{\bf Y}^{(k)}-\eta_{y}{\bf Y}^{(k-1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐘</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝐖𝐘</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝐘</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf Y}^{(k+1)}=(1+\eta_{y}){\bf W}{\bf Y}^{(k)}-\eta_{y}{\bf Y}^{(k-1)}</annotation></semantics></math> 
</div>
<div id="alg1.l5" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">5:</span></span>
            
            
           <span id="alg1.l5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end for</span> 
</div>
<div id="alg1.l6" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l6.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">6:</span></span>
            
            
           <span id="alg1.l6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Output:</span> <math id="alg1.l6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf Y}^{(K)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐘</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf Y}^{(K)}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
</div>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>The Algorithm and Main Results</h2>

<figure id="alg2" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 2</span> </span> Decentralized Stochastic Normalized Gradient Descent (DSNGD)</figcaption>
<div id="alg2.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg2.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">1:</span></span>
            
            
           <span id="alg2.l1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Input:</span> <math id="alg2.l1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bar{\bf{x}}}^{0}\in{\mathbb{R}}^{1\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bar{\bf{x}}}^{0}\in{\mathbb{R}}^{1\times d}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="alg2.l1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf W}\in{\mathbb{R}}^{m\times m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐖</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf W}\in{\mathbb{R}}^{m\times m}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="alg2.l1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="alg2.l1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{K},K,T,b\in{\mathbb{N}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>K</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{K},K,T,b\in{\mathbb{N}}</annotation></semantics></math> 
</div>
<div id="alg2.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">2:</span></span>
            
            
           <math id="alg2.l2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf X}^{0}={\bf{1}}{\bar{\bf{x}}}^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐗</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>𝟏</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf X}^{0}={\bf{1}}{\bar{\bf{x}}}^{0}</annotation></semantics></math>; independently sample <math id="alg2.l2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{\xi}}_{i,k}^{0}\sim{\mathcal{D}}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝝃</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{\xi}}_{i,k}^{0}\sim{\mathcal{D}}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="alg2.l2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\in[b]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\in[b]</annotation></semantics></math> at each agent <math id="alg2.l2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> 
</div>
<div id="alg2.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">3:</span></span>
            
            
            <math id="alg2.l3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf G}^{0}=\begin{bmatrix}\displaystyle{\frac{1}{b}\sum_{k=1}^{b}\nabla F_{1}({\bar{\bf{x}}}^{0};{\bm{\xi}}_{1,k}^{0})}&amp;\dots&amp;\displaystyle{\frac{1}{b}\sum_{k=1}^{b}\nabla F_{m}({\bar{\bf{x}}}^{0};{\bm{\xi}}_{m,k}^{0})}\end{bmatrix}^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐆</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>b</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>b</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝝃</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mtd><mtd><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>b</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>b</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝝃</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf G}^{0}=\begin{bmatrix}\displaystyle{\frac{1}{b}\sum_{k=1}^{b}\nabla F_{1}({\bar{\bf{x}}}^{0};{\bm{\xi}}_{1,k}^{0})}&amp;\dots&amp;\displaystyle{\frac{1}{b}\sum_{k=1}^{b}\nabla F_{m}({\bar{\bf{x}}}^{0};{\bm{\xi}}_{m,k}^{0})}\end{bmatrix}^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math> 
</div>
<div id="alg2.l4" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">4:</span></span>
            
            
            <math id="alg2.l4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf V}^{0}={\rm AccGossip}\big({\bf G}^{0},{\bf W},\hat{K}\big)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐕</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>AccGossip</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐆</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐖</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>K</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf V}^{0}={\rm AccGossip}\big({\bf G}^{0},{\bf W},\hat{K}\big)</annotation></semantics></math> 
</div>
<div id="alg2.l5" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">5:</span></span>
            
            
           <span id="alg2.l5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> <math id="alg2.l5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=0,1,\dots,{T-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=0,1,\dots,{T-1}</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg2.l5.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span> 
</div>
<div id="alg2.l6" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l6.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">6:</span></span>
            
            
             <math id="alg2.l6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf U}^{t}=\begin{bmatrix}\dfrac{{\bf{v}}^{t}_{1}}{\left\|{\bf{v}}^{t}_{1}\right\|}&amp;\dots&amp;\dfrac{{\bf{v}}^{t}_{m}}{\left\|{\bf{v}}^{t}_{m}\right\|}\end{bmatrix}^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐔</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt"><mtr><mtd><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msubsup><mi>𝐯</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐯</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo>‖</mo></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mtd><mtd><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msubsup><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo>‖</mo></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf U}^{t}=\begin{bmatrix}\dfrac{{\bf{v}}^{t}_{1}}{\left\|{\bf{v}}^{t}_{1}\right\|}&amp;\dots&amp;\dfrac{{\bf{v}}^{t}_{m}}{\left\|{\bf{v}}^{t}_{m}\right\|}\end{bmatrix}^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math>  
</div>
<div id="alg2.l7" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l7.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">7:</span></span>
            
            
            <math id="alg2.l7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf X}^{t+1}={\rm AccGossip}\left({\bf X}^{t}-\eta{\bf U}^{t},{\bf W},K\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>AccGossip</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝐔</mi><mi>t</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐖</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf X}^{t+1}={\rm AccGossip}\left({\bf X}^{t}-\eta{\bf U}^{t},{\bf W},K\right)</annotation></semantics></math>  
</div>
<div id="alg2.l8" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l8.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">8:</span></span>
            
            
            independently sample <math id="alg2.l8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{\xi}}_{i,k}^{t+1}\sim{\mathcal{D}}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝝃</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{\xi}}_{i,k}^{t+1}\sim{\mathcal{D}}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="alg2.l8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\in[b]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\in[b]</annotation></semantics></math> at each agent <math id="alg2.l8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l9" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l9.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">9:</span></span>
            
            
             <math id="alg2.l9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf G}^{t+1}=\begin{bmatrix}\displaystyle{\frac{1}{b}\sum_{k=1}^{b}\nabla F_{1}({\bf{x}}_{1}^{t+1};{\bm{\xi}}_{1,k}^{t+1})}&amp;\dots&amp;\displaystyle{\frac{1}{b}\sum_{k=1}^{b}\nabla F_{m}({\bf{x}}_{m}^{t+1};{\bm{\xi}}_{m,k}^{t+1})}\end{bmatrix}^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐆</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>b</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>b</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝝃</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mtd><mtd><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>b</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>b</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝝃</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf G}^{t+1}=\begin{bmatrix}\displaystyle{\frac{1}{b}\sum_{k=1}^{b}\nabla F_{1}({\bf{x}}_{1}^{t+1};{\bm{\xi}}_{1,k}^{t+1})}&amp;\dots&amp;\displaystyle{\frac{1}{b}\sum_{k=1}^{b}\nabla F_{m}({\bf{x}}_{m}^{t+1};{\bm{\xi}}_{m,k}^{t+1})}\end{bmatrix}^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math> 
</div>
<div id="alg2.l10" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l10.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">10:</span></span>
            
            
             <math id="alg2.l10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf V}^{t+1}={\rm AccGossip}\left({\bf V}^{t}+{\bf G}^{t+1}-{\bf G}^{t},{\bf W},K\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐕</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>AccGossip</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝐕</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>𝐆</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>𝐆</mi><mi>t</mi></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐖</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf V}^{t+1}={\rm AccGossip}\left({\bf V}^{t}+{\bf G}^{t+1}-{\bf G}^{t},{\bf W},K\right)</annotation></semantics></math> 
</div>
<div id="alg2.l11" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l11.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">11:</span></span>
            
            
           <span id="alg2.l11.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end for</span> 
</div>
<div id="alg2.l12" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l12.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">12:</span></span>
            
            
           <math id="alg2.l12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\bf{x}}_{i}\sim{\rm Unif}\{{\bf{x}}_{i}^{0},\dots,{\bf{x}}_{i}^{T-1}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>Unif</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\bf{x}}_{i}\sim{\rm Unif}\{{\bf{x}}_{i}^{0},\dots,{\bf{x}}_{i}^{T-1}\}</annotation></semantics></math> for each agent <math id="alg2.l12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>
</div>
<div id="alg2.l13" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l13.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">13:</span></span>
            
            
           <span id="alg2.l13.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Output:</span> <math id="alg2.l13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\bf{x}}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\bf{x}}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> for each agent <math id="alg2.l13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
</div>
</figure>
<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We propose decentralized normalized stochastic gradient
descent (DNSGD) in Algorithm <a href="#alg2" title="Algorithm 2 ‣ 3 The Algorithm and Main Results ‣ Decentralized Stochastic Nonconvex Optimization under the Relaxed Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, which combines the techniques of normalized gradient descent <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>, <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>, <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>, <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>, gradient tracking <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>, <a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>]</cite>, and multi-consensus with Chebyshev acceleration <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>, <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>, <a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">50</a>]</cite>.
Here the matrices <math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf X}^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf X}^{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf V}^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐕</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf V}^{t}</annotation></semantics></math> consists of the local variables and local gradient estimator, i.e., <math id="S3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf X}^{t}=[{\bf{x}}_{1}^{t};\dots;{\bf{x}}_{m}^{t}]^{\top}\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo>;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>;</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf X}^{t}=[{\bf{x}}_{1}^{t};\dots;{\bf{x}}_{m}^{t}]^{\top}\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times d}</annotation></semantics></math>
and
<math id="S3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf V}^{t}=[{\bf{v}}_{1}^{t};\dots;{\bf{v}}_{m}^{t}]^{\top}\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐕</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐯</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo>;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>;</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf V}^{t}=[{\bf{v}}_{1}^{t};\dots;{\bf{v}}_{m}^{t}]^{\top}\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times d}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">It is worth noting that the relaxed
smoothness condition (Assumption <a href="#Thmasm2" title="Assumption 2 (relaxed smoothness). ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Decentralized Stochastic Nonconvex Optimization under the Relaxed Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) does not guarantee that the local gradient is Lipschitz continuous or bounded.
However, the existing analysis of decentralized methods mainly depends on the Lipschitz continuity or the boundedness of the local gradient <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>, <a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">49</a>, <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>, <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>, <a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>, <a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>, <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>, <a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">50</a>, <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>, <a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">52</a>, <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>, <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>, <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>, <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>, <a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>, <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite>, which is not applicable to our setting.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To address above issues, we design the Lyapunov function</p>
<table id="S3.EGx10" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex8"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\Phi^{t}=f({\bar{\bf{x}}}^{t})+\frac{3\eta}{\sqrt{m}}\left(M_{0}+M_{1}\left\|\nabla f({\bar{\bf{x}}}^{t})\right\|\right)\left\|{\bf X}^{t}-{\bf{1}}{\bar{\bf{x}}}^{t}\right\|+\frac{2\eta}{\sqrt{m}}\left\|{\bf V}^{t}-{\bf{1}}{\bar{\bf{v}}}^{t}\right\|," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow><msqrt><mi>m</mi></msqrt></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>𝟏</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow><msqrt><mi>m</mi></msqrt></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐕</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>𝟏</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\Phi^{t}=f({\bar{\bf{x}}}^{t})+\frac{3\eta}{\sqrt{m}}\left(M_{0}+M_{1}\left\|\nabla f({\bar{\bf{x}}}^{t})\right\|\right)\left\|{\bf X}^{t}-{\bf{1}}{\bar{\bf{x}}}^{t}\right\|+\frac{2\eta}{\sqrt{m}}\left\|{\bf V}^{t}-{\bf{1}}{\bar{\bf{v}}}^{t}\right\|,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{0}=\sqrt{2(L_{0}^{2}+L_{1}^{2}\zeta^{2})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ζ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{0}=\sqrt{2(L_{0}^{2}+L_{1}^{2}\zeta^{2})}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{1}=\sqrt{2}L_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{1}=\sqrt{2}L_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>.
In particular, our <math id="S3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> includes the term</p>
<table id="S3.EGx11" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex9"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\left(M_{0}+M_{1}\left\|\nabla f({\bar{\bf{x}}}^{t})\right\|\right)\left\|{\bf X}^{t}-{\bf{1}}{\bar{\bf{x}}}^{t}\right\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>𝟏</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\left(M_{0}+M_{1}\left\|\nabla f({\bar{\bf{x}}}^{t})\right\|\right)\left\|{\bf X}^{t}-{\bf{1}}{\bar{\bf{x}}}^{t}\right\|</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p3.3" class="ltx_p">to align with the measurement unit on the right-hand side of inequality (<a href="#S2.E2" title="In Proposition 1. ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Decentralized Stochastic Nonconvex Optimization under the Relaxed Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) for the relaxed smoothness of the objective.
This design can well address the consensus errors <math id="S3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\|{\bf X}^{t}-{\bf{1}}{\bar{\bf{x}}}^{t}\right\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>𝟏</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\|{\bf X}^{t}-{\bf{1}}{\bar{\bf{x}}}^{t}\right\|</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\|{\bf V}^{t}-{\bf{1}}{\bar{\bf{v}}}^{t}\right\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐕</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>𝟏</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\|{\bf V}^{t}-{\bf{1}}{\bar{\bf{v}}}^{t}\right\|</annotation></semantics></math> even if the local gradient is non-Lipschitz and unbounded.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In contrast, the existing analysis essentially only considers the linear combinations of the function value, gradient norm, and the consensus errors <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>, <a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">49</a>, <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>, <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>, <a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>, <a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>, <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>, <a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">50</a>, <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>, <a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">52</a>, <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>, <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>, <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>, <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>, <a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>, <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite>, so that
it does not work on the relaxed smoothness.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">By using the Lyapunov function <math id="S3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi^{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, we can show the main theoretical results of proposed DSNGD (Algorithm <a href="#alg2" title="Algorithm 2 ‣ 3 The Algorithm and Main Results ‣ Decentralized Stochastic Nonconvex Optimization under the Relaxed Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) as follows.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthm1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1</span></span><span id="Thmthm1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthm1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthm1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Under Assumptions <a href="#Thmasm1" title="Assumption 1 (lower boundedness). ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Decentralized Stochastic Nonconvex Optimization under the Relaxed Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>–<a href="#Thmasm5" title="Assumption 5. ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Decentralized Stochastic Nonconvex Optimization under the Relaxed Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, running DSNGD (Algorithm <a href="#alg2" title="Algorithm 2 ‣ 3 The Algorithm and Main Results ‣ Decentralized Stochastic Nonconvex Optimization under the Relaxed Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) with</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx12" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex10"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\eta=\min\left\{\frac{\epsilon}{4L_{f}+1},\frac{1}{2L_{1}}\right\},\quad b\geq\max\left\{\frac{256(4L_{f}+1)^{2}\sigma^{2}}{mL_{f}^{2}\epsilon^{2}},\frac{1024L_{1}^{2}\sigma^{2}}{mL_{f}^{2}}\right\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>f</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>,</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mn>256</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>f</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>,</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1024</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\eta=\min\left\{\frac{\epsilon}{4L_{f}+1},\frac{1}{2L_{1}}\right\},\quad b\geq\max\left\{\frac{256(4L_{f}+1)^{2}\sigma^{2}}{mL_{f}^{2}\epsilon^{2}},\frac{1024L_{1}^{2}\sigma^{2}}{mL_{f}^{2}}\right\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.Ex11"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle T\geq\max\left\{\frac{8(4L_{f}+1)\Delta_{\Phi}}{\epsilon^{2}},\frac{16L_{1}\Delta_{\Phi}}{\epsilon}\right\},\qquad\text{and}\qquad K={\mathcal{O}}\left(\frac{\log m}{\sqrt{\gamma}}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mn>8</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>f</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi></msub></mrow><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mstyle><mo>,</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mn>16</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi></msub></mrow><mi>ϵ</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">and</mtext><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><msqrt><mi>γ</mi></msqrt></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle T\geq\max\left\{\frac{8(4L_{f}+1)\Delta_{\Phi}}{\epsilon^{2}},\frac{16L_{1}\Delta_{\Phi}}{\epsilon}\right\},\qquad\text{and}\qquad K={\mathcal{O}}\left(\frac{\log m}{\sqrt{\gamma}}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmthm1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthm1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">guarantees <math id="Thmthm1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{E}}\left[\left\|\nabla f(\hat{\bf{x}})\right\|\right]\leq\epsilon/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{E}}\left[\left\|\nabla f(\hat{\bf{x}})\right\|\right]\leq\epsilon/2</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="Thmthm1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\bf{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\bf{x}}</annotation></semantics></math> is uniformly sampled from <math id="Thmthm1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{{\bar{\bf{x}}}^{0},\dots,{\bar{\bf{x}}}^{T-1}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{{\bar{\bf{x}}}^{0},\dots,{\bar{\bf{x}}}^{T-1}\}</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx13" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex12"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle L_{f}=L_{0}+L_{1}\zeta,\qquad\text{and}\qquad\Delta_{\Phi}=f({\bar{\bf{x}}}^{0})-\inf_{{\bf{x}}\in{\mathbb{R}}^{d}}f({\bf{x}})+\frac{2\eta}{\sqrt{m}}{\mathbb{E}}\left[\left\|{\bf V}^{0}-{\bf{1}}{\bar{\bf{v}}}^{0}\right\|\right]." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ζ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">and</mtext><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">inf</mo><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></munder><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow><msqrt><mi>m</mi></msqrt></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐕</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>𝟏</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle L_{f}=L_{0}+L_{1}\zeta,\qquad\text{and}\qquad\Delta_{\Phi}=f({\bar{\bf{x}}}^{0})-\inf_{{\bf{x}}\in{\mathbb{R}}^{d}}f({\bf{x}})+\frac{2\eta}{\sqrt{m}}{\mathbb{E}}\left[\left\|{\bf V}^{0}-{\bf{1}}{\bar{\bf{v}}}^{0}\right\|\right].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Note that the expected <math id="S3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\epsilon/2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\epsilon/2)</annotation></semantics></math>-stationary point <math id="S3.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\bf{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\bf{x}}</annotation></semantics></math> in Theorem <a href="#Thmthm1" title="Theorem 1. ‣ 3 The Algorithm and Main Results ‣ Decentralized Stochastic Nonconvex Optimization under the Relaxed Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> is based on the mean vectors <math id="S3.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{{\bar{\bf{x}}}^{i}\}_{i=1}^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{{\bar{\bf{x}}}^{i}\}_{i=1}^{m}</annotation></semantics></math>, which are unavailable in decentralized setting.
In addition, the expression of <math id="S3.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{\Phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{\Phi}</annotation></semantics></math> indicates the initial consensus error <math id="S3.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\|{\bf V}^{0}-{\bf{1}}{\bar{\bf{v}}}^{0}\right\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐕</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>𝟏</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\|{\bf V}^{0}-{\bf{1}}{\bar{\bf{v}}}^{0}\right\|</annotation></semantics></math> may heavily affect the iteration number <math id="S3.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> if it is significantly larger than <math id="S3.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{f}=f({\bar{\bf{x}}}^{0})-\inf_{{\bf{x}}\in{\mathbb{R}}^{d}}f({\bf{x}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">inf</mo><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{f}=f({\bar{\bf{x}}}^{0})-\inf_{{\bf{x}}\in{\mathbb{R}}^{d}}f({\bf{x}})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Fortunately, we can address above issues by appropriate setting of <math id="S3.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>K</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{K}</annotation></semantics></math> in line <a href="#alg2.l4" title="In Algorithm 2 ‣ 3 The Algorithm and Main Results ‣ Decentralized Stochastic Nonconvex Optimization under the Relaxed Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> to reduce <math id="S3.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\|{\bf V}^{0}-{\bf{1}}{\bar{\bf{v}}}^{0}\right\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐕</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>𝟏</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\|{\bf V}^{0}-{\bf{1}}{\bar{\bf{v}}}^{0}\right\|</annotation></semantics></math> and achieve <math id="S3.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{\Phi}=\Theta(\Delta_{f})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{\Phi}=\Theta(\Delta_{f})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Finally, we achieve the refined upper complexity bounds.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmcor1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_cor">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmcor1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 1</span></span><span id="Thmcor1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmcor1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmcor1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmcor1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Following the setting of Theorem <a href="#Thmthm1" title="Theorem 1. ‣ 3 The Algorithm and Main Results ‣ Decentralized Stochastic Nonconvex Optimization under the Relaxed Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, we further let <math id="Thmcor1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{K}=\tilde{\mathcal{O}}(1/\sqrt{\gamma})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>K</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msqrt><mi>γ</mi></msqrt></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{K}=\tilde{\mathcal{O}}(1/\sqrt{\gamma})</annotation></semantics></math>, then it holds that <math id="Thmcor1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{\Phi}=\Theta(\Delta_{f})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{\Phi}=\Theta(\Delta_{f})</annotation></semantics></math> and the output of DSNGD (Algorithm <a href="#alg2" title="Algorithm 2 ‣ 3 The Algorithm and Main Results ‣ Decentralized Stochastic Nonconvex Optimization under the Relaxed Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) guarantees <math id="Thmcor1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{E}}\left[\left\|\nabla f(\hat{\bf{x}}_{i})\right\|\right]\leq\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{E}}\left[\left\|\nabla f(\hat{\bf{x}}_{i})\right\|\right]\leq\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> for each agent <math id="Thmcor1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in[m]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in[m]</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="Thmcor1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\bf{x}}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\bf{x}}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is uniformly sampled from <math id="Thmcor1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{{\bf{x}}_{i}^{0},\dots,{\bf{x}}_{i}^{T-1}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{{\bf{x}}_{i}^{0},\dots,{\bf{x}}_{i}^{T-1}\}</annotation></semantics></math> generated by the <math id="Thmcor1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>th agent.
Additionally, each agent requires the sample complexity of</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx14" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle Tb={\mathcal{O}}\left(\frac{1}{m}\left(\frac{L_{f}\sigma^{2}\Delta}{\epsilon^{4}}+\frac{\sigma^{2}}{\epsilon^{2}}+\frac{L_{1}^{3}\sigma^{2}\Delta}{L_{f}^{2}\epsilon}+\frac{L_{1}^{2}\sigma^{2}}{L_{f}^{2}}\right)\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>m</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>4</mn></msup></mfrac></mstyle><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mstyle><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle Tb={\mathcal{O}}\left(\frac{1}{m}\left(\frac{L_{f}\sigma^{2}\Delta}{\epsilon^{4}}+\frac{\sigma^{2}}{\epsilon^{2}}+\frac{L_{1}^{3}\sigma^{2}\Delta}{L_{f}^{2}\epsilon}+\frac{L_{1}^{2}\sigma^{2}}{L_{f}^{2}}\right)\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmcor1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmcor1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">and the algorithm requires the communication complexity of</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx15" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex13"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle TK+\hat{K}=\tilde{\mathcal{O}}\left(\frac{L_{f}\Delta_{f}}{\sqrt{\gamma}\epsilon^{2}}+\frac{L_{1}\Delta_{f}}{\sqrt{\gamma}\epsilon}\right)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>K</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mi>γ</mi></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mi>γ</mi></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle TK+\hat{K}=\tilde{\mathcal{O}}\left(\frac{L_{f}\Delta_{f}}{\sqrt{\gamma}\epsilon^{2}}+\frac{L_{1}\Delta_{f}}{\sqrt{\gamma}\epsilon}\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmcor1.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmcor1.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="Thmcor1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{f}=L_{0}+L_{1}\zeta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ζ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{f}=L_{0}+L_{1}\zeta</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmcor1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{f}=f({\bar{\bf{x}}}^{0})-\inf_{{\bf{x}}\in{\mathbb{R}}^{d}}f({\bf{x}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">inf</mo><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{f}=f({\bar{\bf{x}}}^{0})-\inf_{{\bf{x}}\in{\mathbb{R}}^{d}}f({\bf{x}})</annotation></semantics></math>, and the notation <math id="Thmcor1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathcal{O}}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathcal{O}}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> hides the log factors.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p7.1" class="ltx_p">The term <math id="S3.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/m</annotation></semantics></math> in the sample complexity bound (<a href="#S3.E3" title="In Corollary 1. ‣ 3 The Algorithm and Main Results ‣ Decentralized Stochastic Nonconvex Optimization under the Relaxed Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>) implies our method achieves the linear speedup.
Although expression (<a href="#S3.E3" title="In Corollary 1. ‣ 3 The Algorithm and Main Results ‣ Decentralized Stochastic Nonconvex Optimization under the Relaxed Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>) looks somewhat complicated, it is easy to understand since we can verify our bounds match or surpass the best-known results in several special cases:</p>
<ul id="S3.I1" class="ltx_itemize" style="--ltx-enum-leftmargin:0.65cm;">
<li id="S3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the case of <math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{1}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{1}=0</annotation></semantics></math>, the local functions reduce to the setting of standard <math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>-smooth and it leads to <math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{f}=L_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{f}=L_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, since the objective <math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is naturally <math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>-smooth without Assumption <a href="#Thmasm3" title="Assumption 3 (bounded gradient dissimilarity). ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Decentralized Stochastic Nonconvex Optimization under the Relaxed Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>. Then Corollary <a href="#Thmcor1" title="Corollary 1. ‣ 3 The Algorithm and Main Results ‣ Decentralized Stochastic Nonconvex Optimization under the Relaxed Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> indicates the sample complexity and the communication complexity of</p>
<table id="S3.EGx16" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex14"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle Tb={\mathcal{O}}\left(\frac{1}{m}\left(\frac{L_{0}\sigma^{2}\Delta_{f}}{\epsilon^{4}}+\frac{\sigma^{2}}{\epsilon^{2}}\right)\right)\qquad\text{and}\qquad TK+\hat{K}=\tilde{\mathcal{O}}\left(\frac{L_{0}\Delta_{f}}{\gamma^{1/2}\epsilon^{2}}\right)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>m</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub></mrow><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>4</mn></msup></mfrac></mstyle><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mtext>and</mtext><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>K</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>γ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle Tb={\mathcal{O}}\left(\frac{1}{m}\left(\frac{L_{0}\sigma^{2}\Delta_{f}}{\epsilon^{4}}+\frac{\sigma^{2}}{\epsilon^{2}}\right)\right)\qquad\text{and}\qquad TK+\hat{K}=\tilde{\mathcal{O}}\left(\frac{L_{0}\Delta_{f}}{\gamma^{1/2}\epsilon^{2}}\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.I1.i1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">respectively. This matches the (near-)optimal results for decentralized stochastic nonconvex optimization under the standard smoothness <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>, Corollary 1]</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the case of <math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=1</annotation></semantics></math>, the problem reduces to the relaxed smooth optimization on single machine. This leads to <math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\zeta=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ζ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\zeta=0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{f}=L_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{f}=L_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> since there is no heterogeneity.
Then Theorem <a href="#Thmthm1" title="Theorem 1. ‣ 3 The Algorithm and Main Results ‣ Decentralized Stochastic Nonconvex Optimization under the Relaxed Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> indicates the sample complexity of</p>
<table id="S3.EGx17" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle Tb={\mathcal{O}}\left(\frac{L_{0}\sigma^{2}\Delta}{\epsilon^{4}}+\frac{\sigma^{2}}{\epsilon^{2}}+\frac{L_{1}^{3}\sigma^{2}\Delta}{L_{0}^{2}\epsilon}+\frac{L_{1}^{2}\sigma^{2}}{L_{0}^{2}}\right)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>4</mn></msup></mfrac></mstyle><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mstyle><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle Tb={\mathcal{O}}\left(\frac{L_{0}\sigma^{2}\Delta}{\epsilon^{4}}+\frac{\sigma^{2}}{\epsilon^{2}}+\frac{L_{1}^{3}\sigma^{2}\Delta}{L_{0}^{2}\epsilon}+\frac{L_{1}^{2}\sigma^{2}}{L_{0}^{2}}\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">which is tighter than the complexity of gradient clipping method in the non-distributed case <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">53</a>]</cite>. Recall that Theorem 3.2 of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al. [53]</a></cite> claims the sample complexity of <math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{O}}(L_{0}\sigma^{2}\Delta\epsilon^{-4}+L_{0}^{-3}L_{1}^{4}\sigma^{2}\Delta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>4</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{O}}(L_{0}\sigma^{2}\Delta\epsilon^{-4}+L_{0}^{-3}L_{1}^{4}\sigma^{2}\Delta)</annotation></semantics></math>.
It is worth noting that term <math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{0}^{-3}L_{1}^{4}\sigma^{2}\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>4</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{0}^{-3}L_{1}^{4}\sigma^{2}\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> dominates their bound only when <math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon=\Omega(L_{0}/L_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon=\Omega(L_{0}/L_{1})</annotation></semantics></math>, and it is larger than the term <math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{0}^{-2}L_{1}^{3}\sigma^{2}\Delta\epsilon^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{0}^{-2}L_{1}^{3}\sigma^{2}\Delta\epsilon^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math> in our (<a href="#S3.E4" title="In 2nd item ‣ 3 The Algorithm and Main Results ‣ Decentralized Stochastic Nonconvex Optimization under the Relaxed Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>) in this case.
In addition, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al. [53]</a></cite> assume the algorithm can access the exact gradient at the initial point, while their complexity bound does not include the cost of achieving the the exact gradient at the initial point.
In contrast, our analysis considers the access of the initial gradient estimator by stochastic first-order oracle, leading to the terms <math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{2}\epsilon^{-2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{2}\epsilon^{-2}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{1}^{2}L_{0}^{-2}\sigma^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{1}^{2}L_{0}^{-2}\sigma^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> in our upper bound (<a href="#S3.E4" title="In 2nd item ‣ 3 The Algorithm and Main Results ‣ Decentralized Stochastic Nonconvex Optimization under the Relaxed Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>).
Recently, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu &amp; Zhou [30]</a></cite> study the more general stochastic gradient estimator with heavy-tail noise, while applying their result to our setting leads to the term <math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{3}\epsilon^{-3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{3}\epsilon^{-3}</annotation></semantics></math> in the upper bound on the sample complexity <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>, Theorem 3.2 with <math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=2</annotation></semantics></math>]</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Following the case of <math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=1</annotation></semantics></math>, we further consider the deterministic case, i.e., replace the mini-batch stochastic gradient in Algorithm <a href="#alg2" title="Algorithm 2 ‣ 3 The Algorithm and Main Results ‣ Decentralized Stochastic Nonconvex Optimization under the Relaxed Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> with the exact gradient and omit the communication steps.
Then Theorem <a href="#Thmthm1" title="Theorem 1. ‣ 3 The Algorithm and Main Results ‣ Decentralized Stochastic Nonconvex Optimization under the Relaxed Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> indicates the iteration (gradient) complexity of</p>
<table id="S3.EGx18" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex15"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle T={\mathcal{O}}\left(\frac{L_{0}\Delta_{f}}{\epsilon^{2}}+\frac{L_{1}\Delta_{f}}{\epsilon}\right)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub></mrow><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mstyle><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub></mrow><mi>ϵ</mi></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle T={\mathcal{O}}\left(\frac{L_{0}\Delta_{f}}{\epsilon^{2}}+\frac{L_{1}\Delta_{f}}{\epsilon}\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.I1.i3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">matching the best-known result for solving relaxed smooth problem by exact gradient method on single machine <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">46</a>, Theorem 3.1]</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>The Complexity Analysis</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we briefly sketch the derivations of our main results. The details for all proofs are presented in appendices.
We first present the descent lemma with respect to the mean vectors <math id="S4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bar{\bf{x}}}^{t+1}=\frac{1}{m}\sum_{i=1}^{m}{\bf{x}}_{i}^{t+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>m</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bar{\bf{x}}}^{t+1}=\frac{1}{m}\sum_{i=1}^{m}{\bf{x}}_{i}^{t+1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bar{\bf{x}}}^{t}=\frac{1}{m}\sum_{i=1}^{m}{\bf{x}}_{i}^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>m</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bar{\bf{x}}}^{t}=\frac{1}{m}\sum_{i=1}^{m}{\bf{x}}_{i}^{t}</annotation></semantics></math> as follows.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmlem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmlem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 1</span></span><span id="Thmlem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmlem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmlem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmlem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Under the setting of Theorem <a href="#Thmthm1" title="Theorem 1. ‣ 3 The Algorithm and Main Results ‣ Decentralized Stochastic Nonconvex Optimization under the Relaxed Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, we have</span></p>
<table id="S4.EGx19" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex16"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle{\mathbb{E}}[f({\bar{\bf{x}}}^{t+1})]\leq" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle{\mathbb{E}}[f({\bar{\bf{x}}}^{t+1})]\leq</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex16.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle f({\bar{\bf{x}}}^{t})-\eta\left(1-\frac{\eta L_{1}}{2}\right)\left\|\nabla f({\bar{\bf{x}}}^{t})\right\|+\frac{2\sqrt{2}\eta}{\sqrt{m}}\left(M_{0}+M_{1}\left\|\nabla f({\bar{\bf{x}}}^{t})\right\|\right)\left\|{\bf X}^{t}-{\bf{1}}{\bar{\bf{x}}}^{t}\right\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow><msqrt><mi>m</mi></msqrt></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>𝟏</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle f({\bar{\bf{x}}}^{t})-\eta\left(1-\frac{\eta L_{1}}{2}\right)\left\|\nabla f({\bar{\bf{x}}}^{t})\right\|+\frac{2\sqrt{2}\eta}{\sqrt{m}}\left(M_{0}+M_{1}\left\|\nabla f({\bar{\bf{x}}}^{t})\right\|\right)\left\|{\bf X}^{t}-{\bf{1}}{\bar{\bf{x}}}^{t}\right\|</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex17"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle+\frac{\eta}{\sqrt{m}}\left\|{\bf V}^{t}-{\bf{1}}{\bar{\bf{v}}}^{t}\right\|+\frac{2\eta\sigma}{\sqrt{mb}}+\frac{\eta^{2}L_{f}}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>η</mi><msqrt><mi>m</mi></msqrt></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐕</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>𝟏</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>η</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow><msqrt><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msqrt></mfrac></mstyle><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>η</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>f</mi></msub></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle+\frac{\eta}{\sqrt{m}}\left\|{\bf V}^{t}-{\bf{1}}{\bar{\bf{v}}}^{t}\right\|+\frac{2\eta\sigma}{\sqrt{mb}}+\frac{\eta^{2}L_{f}}{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmlem1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmlem1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">for given <math id="Thmlem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf X}^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf X}^{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmlem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf V}^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐕</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf V}^{t}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We define <math id="S4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho=c_{1}(1-c_{2}\sqrt{1-\lambda_{2}({\bf W})}\,)^{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐖</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>K</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho=c_{1}(1-c_{2}\sqrt{1-\lambda_{2}({\bf W})}\,)^{K}</annotation></semantics></math>
with <math id="S4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{1}=\sqrt{14}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mn>14</mn></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{1}=\sqrt{14}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{2}=1-1/\sqrt{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{2}=1-1/\sqrt{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Proposition <a href="#Thmprop2" title="Proposition 2 ( [ , Proposition 1] ). ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Decentralized Stochastic Nonconvex Optimization under the Relaxed Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> implies we can guarantee <math id="S4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> be sufficient small by taking appropriate communication rounds number <math id="S4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>.
We then establish the recursions for the consensus errors as follows.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmlem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmlem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 2</span></span><span id="Thmlem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmlem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmlem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmlem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For Algorithm <a href="#alg2" title="Algorithm 2 ‣ 3 The Algorithm and Main Results ‣ Decentralized Stochastic Nonconvex Optimization under the Relaxed Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, we have</span></p>
<table id="S4.EGx20" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex18"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\left\|{\bf X}^{t+1}-{\bf{1}}{\bar{\bf{x}}}^{t+1}\right\|\leq\rho\left(\left\|{\bf X}^{t}-{\bf{1}}{\bar{\bf{x}}}^{t}\right\|+m\eta\right)\qquad\text{and}\qquad\left\|{\bf X}^{t}-{\bf{1}}{\bar{\bf{x}}}^{t}\right\|\leq\frac{\rho m\eta}{1-\rho}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>𝟏</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>𝟏</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">and</mtext><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>𝟏</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\left\|{\bf X}^{t+1}-{\bf{1}}{\bar{\bf{x}}}^{t+1}\right\|\leq\rho\left(\left\|{\bf X}^{t}-{\bf{1}}{\bar{\bf{x}}}^{t}\right\|+m\eta\right)\qquad\text{and}\qquad\left\|{\bf X}^{t}-{\bf{1}}{\bar{\bf{x}}}^{t}\right\|\leq\frac{\rho m\eta}{1-\rho}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmlem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmlem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 3</span></span><span id="Thmlem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmlem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmlem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmlem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For Algorithm <a href="#alg2" title="Algorithm 2 ‣ 3 The Algorithm and Main Results ‣ Decentralized Stochastic Nonconvex Optimization under the Relaxed Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> with <math id="Thmlem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K\geq\sqrt{1/\gamma}\log(1+m\eta L_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>η</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K\geq\sqrt{1/\gamma}\log(1+m\eta L_{1})</annotation></semantics></math>,
we have</span></p>
<table id="S4.EGx21" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex19"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td colspan="2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\small\begin{split}&{\mathbb{E}}\left[\left\|{\bf V}^{t+1}-{\bf{1}}{\bar{\bf{v}}}^{t+1}\right\|\right]\\
\leq&amp;\rho\left(\left\|{\bf V}^{t}-{\bf{1}}{\bar{\bf{v}}}^{t}\right\|+\left(M_{2}+M_{3}\left\|\nabla f({\bar{\bf{x}}}^{t})\right\|\right)\left\|{\bf X}^{t}-{\bf{1}}{\bar{\bf{x}}}^{t}\right\|+\left(M_{2}+M_{3}\left\|\nabla f({\bar{\bf{x}}}^{t})\right\|\right)m\eta+\frac{2\sqrt{m}\sigma}{\sqrt{b}}\right)\!,\!\!\end{split}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐕</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">+</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></mrow></msup><mo mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><mrow><mn mathsize="0.900em">𝟏</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐯</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">+</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mo mathsize="0.900em">≤</mo></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐕</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi></msup><mo mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><mrow><mn mathsize="0.900em">𝟏</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐯</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">M</mi><mn mathsize="0.900em">2</mn></msub><mo mathsize="0.900em">+</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">M</mi><mn mathsize="0.900em">3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">f</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐱</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi></msup><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐗</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi></msup><mo mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><mrow><mn mathsize="0.900em">𝟏</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐱</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">M</mi><mn mathsize="0.900em">2</mn></msub><mo mathsize="0.900em">+</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">M</mi><mn mathsize="0.900em">3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">f</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐱</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi></msup><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">η</mi></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mn mathsize="0.900em">2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mi mathsize="0.900em">m</mi></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">σ</mi></mrow><msqrt><mi mathsize="0.900em">b</mi></msqrt></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mpadded style="width:0.089em;" width="0.089em"><mo>)</mo></mpadded></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\small\begin{split}&amp;{\mathbb{E}}\left[\left\|{\bf V}^{t+1}-{\bf{1}}{\bar{\bf{v}}}^{t+1}\right\|\right]\\
\leq&amp;\rho\left(\left\|{\bf V}^{t}-{\bf{1}}{\bar{\bf{v}}}^{t}\right\|+\left(M_{2}+M_{3}\left\|\nabla f({\bar{\bf{x}}}^{t})\right\|\right)\left\|{\bf X}^{t}-{\bf{1}}{\bar{\bf{x}}}^{t}\right\|+\left(M_{2}+M_{3}\left\|\nabla f({\bar{\bf{x}}}^{t})\right\|\right)m\eta+\frac{2\sqrt{m}\sigma}{\sqrt{b}}\right)\!,\!\!\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmlem3.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmlem3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">for given <math id="Thmlem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf X}^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf X}^{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmlem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf V}^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐕</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf V}^{t}</annotation></semantics></math>,
where <math id="Thmlem3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{2}=(\rho+1)L_{f}+\rho L_{f}L_{1}\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>f</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{2}=(\rho+1)L_{f}+\rho L_{f}L_{1}\eta</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="Thmlem3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{3}=\rho L_{1}(L_{1}\eta+1)+L_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{3}=\rho L_{1}(L_{1}\eta+1)+L_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Applying Lemmas <a href="#Thmlem1" title="Lemma 1. ‣ 4 The Complexity Analysis ‣ Decentralized Stochastic Nonconvex Optimization under the Relaxed Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, <a href="#Thmlem2" title="Lemma 2. ‣ 4 The Complexity Analysis ‣ Decentralized Stochastic Nonconvex Optimization under the Relaxed Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, and <a href="#Thmlem3" title="Lemma 3. ‣ 4 The Complexity Analysis ‣ Decentralized Stochastic Nonconvex Optimization under the Relaxed Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, we can characterize the decease of the Lyapunov function as</p>
<table id="S4.EGx22" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle{\mathbb{E}}[\Phi^{t+1}]\leq\Phi^{t}-\frac{5\eta}{8}\left\|\nabla f({\bar{\bf{x}}}^{t})\right\|+\frac{3\eta^{2}L_{f}}{4}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow><mn>8</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>η</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>f</mi></msub></mrow><mn>4</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle{\mathbb{E}}[\Phi^{t+1}]\leq\Phi^{t}-\frac{5\eta}{8}\left\|\nabla f({\bar{\bf{x}}}^{t})\right\|+\frac{3\eta^{2}L_{f}}{4}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p3.2" class="ltx_p">Summing equation (<a href="#S4.E5" title="In 4 The Complexity Analysis ‣ Decentralized Stochastic Nonconvex Optimization under the Relaxed Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>) over <math id="S4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=0,\dots,T-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=0,\dots,T-1</annotation></semantics></math>,
we can achieve our main result presented in Theorem <a href="#Thmthm1" title="Theorem 1. ‣ 3 The Algorithm and Main Results ‣ Decentralized Stochastic Nonconvex Optimization under the Relaxed Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> that
guarantees <math id="S4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{E}}\left[\left\|\nabla f(\hat{\bf{x}})\right\|\right]\leq\epsilon/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{E}}\left[\left\|\nabla f(\hat{\bf{x}})\right\|\right]\leq\epsilon/2</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\bf{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\bf{x}}</annotation></semantics></math> is uniformly sampled from <math id="S4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{{\bar{\bf{x}}}^{1},\dots,{\bar{\bf{x}}}^{m}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>m</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{{\bar{\bf{x}}}^{1},\dots,{\bar{\bf{x}}}^{m}\}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In Theorem <a href="#Thmthm1" title="Theorem 1. ‣ 3 The Algorithm and Main Results ‣ Decentralized Stochastic Nonconvex Optimization under the Relaxed Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, the setting of iterations number
<math id="S4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\geq\max\left\{8(4L_{f}+1)\Delta_{\Phi}\epsilon^{-2},16L_{1}\Delta_{\Phi}\epsilon^{-1}\right\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mn>8</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>f</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>16</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\geq\max\left\{8(4L_{f}+1)\Delta_{\Phi}\epsilon^{-2},16L_{1}\Delta_{\Phi}\epsilon^{-1}\right\}</annotation></semantics></math> depends on the term
<math id="S4.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{\Phi}=f({\bar{\bf{x}}}^{0})-\inf_{{\bf{x}}\in{\mathbb{R}}^{d}}f({\bf{x}})+(2\eta/\sqrt{m}\,){\mathbb{E}}\left[\left\|{\bf V}^{0}-{\bf{1}}{\bar{\bf{v}}}^{0}\right\|\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">inf</mo><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><msqrt><mi>m</mi></msqrt></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐕</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>𝟏</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{\Phi}=f({\bar{\bf{x}}}^{0})-\inf_{{\bf{x}}\in{\mathbb{R}}^{d}}f({\bf{x}})+(2\eta/\sqrt{m}\,){\mathbb{E}}\left[\left\|{\bf V}^{0}-{\bf{1}}{\bar{\bf{v}}}^{0}\right\|\right]</annotation></semantics></math>, which may be dominated by the expected consensus error <math id="S4.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{E}}\left[\left\|{\bf V}^{0}-{\bf{1}}{\bar{\bf{v}}}^{0}\right\|\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐕</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>𝟏</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{E}}\left[\left\|{\bf V}^{0}-{\bf{1}}{\bar{\bf{v}}}^{0}\right\|\right]</annotation></semantics></math>.
However, the results in the single machine scenario only depend on <math id="S4.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{f}=f({\bar{\bf{x}}}^{0})-\inf_{{\bf{x}}\in{\mathbb{R}}^{d}}f({\bf{x}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">inf</mo><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{f}=f({\bar{\bf{x}}}^{0})-\inf_{{\bf{x}}\in{\mathbb{R}}^{d}}f({\bf{x}})</annotation></semantics></math>.
To fill this gap, we set the number of communication rounds at to be <math id="S4.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{K}=\tilde{\mathcal{O}}(1/\sqrt{\gamma})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>K</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msqrt><mi>γ</mi></msqrt></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{K}=\tilde{\mathcal{O}}(1/\sqrt{\gamma})</annotation></semantics></math> for line <a href="#alg2.l4" title="In Algorithm 2 ‣ 3 The Algorithm and Main Results ‣ Decentralized Stochastic Nonconvex Optimization under the Relaxed Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> of Algorithm <a href="#alg2" title="Algorithm 2 ‣ 3 The Algorithm and Main Results ‣ Decentralized Stochastic Nonconvex Optimization under the Relaxed Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, which guarantees that the consensus error satisfies <math id="S4.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(2\eta/\sqrt{m}\,){\mathbb{E}}\left[\left\|{\bf V}^{0}-{\bf{1}}{\bar{\bf{v}}}^{0}\right\|\right]\leq{\mathcal{O}}(\Delta_{f})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><msqrt><mi>m</mi></msqrt></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐕</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>𝟏</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(2\eta/\sqrt{m}\,){\mathbb{E}}\left[\left\|{\bf V}^{0}-{\bf{1}}{\bar{\bf{v}}}^{0}\right\|\right]\leq{\mathcal{O}}(\Delta_{f})</annotation></semantics></math> and achieves <math id="S4.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{\Phi}\leq{\mathcal{O}}(\Delta_{f})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{\Phi}\leq{\mathcal{O}}(\Delta_{f})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Therefore, the upper bound of <math id="S4.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> to guarantee <math id="S4.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{E}}\left[\left\|\nabla f(\hat{\bf{x}})\right\|\right]\leq\epsilon/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{E}}\left[\left\|\nabla f(\hat{\bf{x}})\right\|\right]\leq\epsilon/2</annotation></semantics></math> can be improved to
<math id="S4.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{O}}(L_{f}\Delta_{f}\epsilon^{-2}+L_{1}\Delta_{f}\epsilon^{-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{O}}(L_{f}\Delta_{f}\epsilon^{-2}+L_{1}\Delta_{f}\epsilon^{-1})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p5.1" class="ltx_p">For achieving an <math id="S4.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-stationary point on each node,
we consider the gradient at <math id="S4.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\bf{x}}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\bf{x}}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> as follows</p>
<table id="S4.EGx23" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex20"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle{\mathbb{E}}[\left\|\nabla f(\hat{\bf{x}}_{i})\right\|]={\mathbb{E}}\left[\frac{1}{T}\sum_{t=0}^{T-1}\left\|\nabla f({\bf{x}}_{i}^{t})\right\|\right]\leq{\mathbb{E}}\bigg[\underbrace{\frac{1}{T}\sum_{t=0}^{T-1}\left\|\nabla f({\bf{x}}_{i}^{t})-\nabla f({\bar{\bf{x}}}^{t})\right\|}_{A_{1}}+\underbrace{\frac{1}{T}\sum_{t=0}^{T-1}\left\|\nabla f({\bar{\bf{x}}}^{t})\right\|}_{A_{2}}\bigg]." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>T</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">[</mo><mrow><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>T</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></munder><mo>+</mo><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>T</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></munder></mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle{\mathbb{E}}[\left\|\nabla f(\hat{\bf{x}}_{i})\right\|]={\mathbb{E}}\left[\frac{1}{T}\sum_{t=0}^{T-1}\left\|\nabla f({\bf{x}}_{i}^{t})\right\|\right]\leq{\mathbb{E}}\bigg[\underbrace{\frac{1}{T}\sum_{t=0}^{T-1}\left\|\nabla f({\bf{x}}_{i}^{t})-\nabla f({\bar{\bf{x}}}^{t})\right\|}_{A_{1}}+\underbrace{\frac{1}{T}\sum_{t=0}^{T-1}\left\|\nabla f({\bar{\bf{x}}}^{t})\right\|}_{A_{2}}\bigg].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p5.2" class="ltx_p">For term <math id="S4.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, Assumption <a href="#Thmasm2" title="Assumption 2 (relaxed smoothness). ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Decentralized Stochastic Nonconvex Optimization under the Relaxed Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> implies</p>
<table id="S4.EGx24" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex21"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle{\mathbb{E}}[A_{1}]\leq{\mathbb{E}}\left[\frac{1}{T}\sum_{t=0}^{T-1}\big(L_{f}+L_{1}\left\|\nabla f({\bar{\bf{x}}}^{t})\right\|\big)\left\|{\bf X}^{t}-{\bf{1}}{\bar{\bf{x}}}^{t}\right\|\right]\leq{\mathbb{E}}\left[\frac{1}{T}\sum_{t=0}^{T-1}\frac{\rho m\eta\big(L_{f}+L_{1}TB\big)}{1-\rho}\right]," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>T</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>𝟏</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>T</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>η</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle{\mathbb{E}}[A_{1}]\leq{\mathbb{E}}\left[\frac{1}{T}\sum_{t=0}^{T-1}\big(L_{f}+L_{1}\left\|\nabla f({\bar{\bf{x}}}^{t})\right\|\big)\left\|{\bf X}^{t}-{\bf{1}}{\bar{\bf{x}}}^{t}\right\|\right]\leq{\mathbb{E}}\left[\frac{1}{T}\sum_{t=0}^{T-1}\frac{\rho m\eta\big(L_{f}+L_{1}TB\big)}{1-\rho}\right],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p5.3" class="ltx_p">where the last step is based on Lemma <a href="#Thmlem8" title="Lemma 8. ‣ Appendix C The Proof of Lemma ‣ Decentralized Stochastic Nonconvex Optimization under the Relaxed Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>.
Recall that Theorem <a href="#Thmthm1" title="Theorem 1. ‣ 3 The Algorithm and Main Results ‣ Decentralized Stochastic Nonconvex Optimization under the Relaxed Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> shows <math id="S4.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{E}}[A_{2}]={\mathbb{E}}[\left\|\nabla f(\hat{\bf{x}})\right\|]\leq\epsilon/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{E}}[A_{2}]={\mathbb{E}}[\left\|\nabla f(\hat{\bf{x}})\right\|]\leq\epsilon/2</annotation></semantics></math>,
then we can set appropriate <math id="S4.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=\tilde{\mathcal{O}}(\sqrt{1/\gamma}\,)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo lspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=\tilde{\mathcal{O}}(\sqrt{1/\gamma}\,)</annotation></semantics></math> to make <math id="S4.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> be sufficient small to guarantee <math id="S4.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{E}}[A_{1}]\leq\epsilon/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{E}}[A_{1}]\leq\epsilon/2</annotation></semantics></math>.
Therefore, we achieve the result of Corollary <a href="#Thmcor1" title="Corollary 1. ‣ 3 The Algorithm and Main Results ‣ Decentralized Stochastic Nonconvex Optimization under the Relaxed Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Discussion and Related Work</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The design of our DSNGD (Algorithm <a href="#alg2" title="Algorithm 2 ‣ 3 The Algorithm and Main Results ‣ Decentralized Stochastic Nonconvex Optimization under the Relaxed Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) is based on normalized (stochastic) gradient descent, which is extensively studied in centralized scenario <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>, <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>, <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>, <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>, <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>.
Recently, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al. [25]</a></cite> studied normalized stochastic gradient descent in decentralized setting, while their analysis is only limited to the setting of standard smoothness.
In a very recent work, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jiang et al. [17, Algorithm 2 and Theorem 4]</a></cite> claims a first-order method for decentralized optimization under the relaxed smoothness, while each of its iteration requires every node to perform the step of gradient clipping which depends on accessing the mean vector <math id="S5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bar{\bf{x}}}_{i}^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bar{\bf{x}}}_{i}^{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and the corresponding exact local gradient <math id="S5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla f_{i}({\bar{\bf{x}}}^{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla f_{i}({\bar{\bf{x}}}^{t})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Recall that the decentralized method typically only allows each node directly communicates with its neighbors (Assumption <a href="#Thmasm5" title="Assumption 5. ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Decentralized Stochastic Nonconvex Optimization under the Relaxed Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>) and the stochastic first-order oracle can only access the unbiased gradient estimator with bounded variance (Assumption <a href="#Thmasm4" title="Assumption 4. ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Decentralized Stochastic Nonconvex Optimization under the Relaxed Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>).
Therefore, we argue that their algorithm is not truly decentralized or stochastic.
In contrast, our method only requires each node to iterate with its local stochastic gradient (line <a href="#alg2.l9" title="In Algorithm 2 ‣ 3 The Algorithm and Main Results ‣ Decentralized Stochastic Nonconvex Optimization under the Relaxed Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a> of Algorithm <a href="#alg2" title="Algorithm 2 ‣ 3 The Algorithm and Main Results ‣ Decentralized Stochastic Nonconvex Optimization under the Relaxed Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>), which is fully decentralized and stochastic.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The convergence analysis for our DSNGD (Algorithm <a href="#alg2" title="Algorithm 2 ‣ 3 The Algorithm and Main Results ‣ Decentralized Stochastic Nonconvex Optimization under the Relaxed Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) depends on the assumption of bounded gradient dissimilarity (Assumption <a href="#Thmasm3" title="Assumption 3 (bounded gradient dissimilarity). ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Decentralized Stochastic Nonconvex Optimization under the Relaxed Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>), which is commonly used in distributed optimization <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">47</a>, <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite>.
Specifically, we use this assumption to establish the inequality
<math id="S5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\|\nabla f({\bar{\bf{x}}}^{t+1})\right\|\leq(L_{0}+L_{1}\zeta)\eta+\left(1+L_{1}\eta\right)\left\|\nabla f({\bar{\bf{x}}}^{t})\right\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ζ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\|\nabla f({\bar{\bf{x}}}^{t+1})\right\|\leq(L_{0}+L_{1}\zeta)\eta+\left(1+L_{1}\eta\right)\left\|\nabla f({\bar{\bf{x}}}^{t})\right\|</annotation></semantics></math>,
which connects the gradients at points gradients at points <math id="S5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bar{\bf{x}}}^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bar{\bf{x}}}^{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bar{\bf{x}}}^{t+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bar{\bf{x}}}^{t+1}</annotation></semantics></math> (see Lemma <a href="#Thmlem9" title="Lemma 9. ‣ Appendix C The Proof of Lemma ‣ Decentralized Stochastic Nonconvex Optimization under the Relaxed Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a> in Appendix <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C The Proof of Lemma ‣ Decentralized Stochastic Nonconvex Optimization under the Relaxed Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>).
We also combine Assumption <a href="#Thmasm3" title="Assumption 3 (bounded gradient dissimilarity). ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Decentralized Stochastic Nonconvex Optimization under the Relaxed Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> with the <math id="S5.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(L_{0},L_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(L_{0},L_{1})</annotation></semantics></math>-smoothness of local functions (Assumption <a href="#Thmasm2" title="Assumption 2 (relaxed smoothness). ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Decentralized Stochastic Nonconvex Optimization under the Relaxed Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) to guarantee the global objective <math id="S5.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f=\frac{1}{m}\sum_{i=1}^{m}f_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>m</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f=\frac{1}{m}\sum_{i=1}^{m}f_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S5.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(L_{0}+L_{1}\zeta,L_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ζ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(L_{0}+L_{1}\zeta,L_{1})</annotation></semantics></math>-smooth (Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B The Proof of Proposition ‣ Decentralized Stochastic Nonconvex Optimization under the Relaxed Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>).
How to establish the convergence for decentralized relaxed smooth optimization without Assumption <a href="#Thmasm3" title="Assumption 3 (bounded gradient dissimilarity). ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Decentralized Stochastic Nonconvex Optimization under the Relaxed Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> is still an open problem.
It is worth noting that simply assuming that each local function <math id="S5.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S5.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(L_{0},L_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(L_{0},L_{1})</annotation></semantics></math>-smooth even cannot guarantee that the global function <math id="S5.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f=\frac{1}{m}\sum_{i=1}^{m}f_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>m</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f=\frac{1}{m}\sum_{i=1}^{m}f_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is also <math id="S5.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(L_{0},L_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(L_{0},L_{1})</annotation></semantics></math>-smooth, which is different with the standard smooth case.
We illustrate this point with the following example.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmprop3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmprop3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 3</span></span><span id="Thmprop3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmprop3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmprop3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmprop3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">We consider functions</span></p>
<table id="S5.EGx25" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S5.Ex22"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex22.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle f_{1}(x)=\exp(Lx),\quad f_{2}(x)=\exp(-Lx),\quad\text{and}\quad f(x)=\frac{f_{1}(x)+f_{2}(x)}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>exp</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>exp</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>L</mi><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">and</mtext><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle f_{1}(x)=\exp(Lx),\quad f_{2}(x)=\exp(-Lx),\quad\text{and}\quad f(x)=\frac{f_{1}(x)+f_{2}(x)}{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmprop3.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmprop3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">for some <math id="Thmprop3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> and let <math id="Thmprop3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{1}=L/\log 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>/</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{1}=L/\log 2</annotation></semantics></math>, then both functions <math id="Thmprop3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmprop3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="Thmprop3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(0,L_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(0,L_{1})</annotation></semantics></math>-smooth while the function <math id="Thmprop3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is not <math id="Thmprop3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(0,L_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(0,L_{1})</annotation></semantics></math>-smooth.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Recall that the discussion after Corollary <a href="#Thmcor1" title="Corollary 1. ‣ 3 The Algorithm and Main Results ‣ Decentralized Stochastic Nonconvex Optimization under the Relaxed Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> shows that the sample complexity of our method matches the lower bound in the special case of <math id="S5.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{1}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{1}=0</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite>.
This implies that the <math id="S5.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{O}}(\epsilon^{-4})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{O}}(\epsilon^{-4})</annotation></semantics></math> dependence on accuracy we obtained cannot be improved in our setting.
If we target to achieve an <math id="S5.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(\epsilon^{-3})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(\epsilon^{-3})</annotation></semantics></math> dependence in the sample complexity, the assumption of expected relaxed smoothness is necessary <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>, <a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>]</cite>, e.g., strengthen
condition (<a href="#S2.E1" title="In Assumption 2 (relaxed smoothness). ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Decentralized Stochastic Nonconvex Optimization under the Relaxed Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) in Assumption <a href="#Thmasm2" title="Assumption 2 (relaxed smoothness). ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Decentralized Stochastic Nonconvex Optimization under the Relaxed Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> to
<math id="S5.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{E}}[\left\|\nabla F_{i}({\bf{x}};{\bm{\xi}}_{i})-\nabla F_{i}({\bf{y}};{\bm{\xi}}_{i})\right\|^{2}]\leq\left(L_{0}+L_{1}\left\|\nabla f_{i}({\bf{x}})\right\|\right)^{2}\left\|{\bf{x}}-{\bf{y}}\right\|^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝝃</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝝃</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>𝐲</mi></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{E}}[\left\|\nabla F_{i}({\bf{x}};{\bm{\xi}}_{i})-\nabla F_{i}({\bf{y}};{\bm{\xi}}_{i})\right\|^{2}]\leq\left(L_{0}+L_{1}\left\|\nabla f_{i}({\bf{x}})\right\|\right)^{2}\left\|{\bf{x}}-{\bf{y}}\right\|^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>,
then applying the variance-reduced gradient estimators <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>, <a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>]</cite> on local agents is possible to achieve the sample complexity depends on <math id="S5.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(\epsilon^{-3})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(\epsilon^{-3})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Experiments</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we empirically compare our decentralized normalized stochastic gradient descent (DNSGD) with baseline methods, including decentralized stochastic gradient descent (D-SGD) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite>, decentralized stochastic gradient descent with gradient tracking (D-SGT) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>, <a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>]</cite>, and decentralized normalized averaged stochastic approximation (D-NASA) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite>. The experiments are performed on a virtualized cluster with 8 vGPUs (NVIDIA RTX 5090, 25.6 GB memory), 8 vCPUs (Intel Xeon Gold 6530), and 96 GB memory.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We present the numerical results on the application of image classification for the MNIST dataset.
Specifically we train a three-layer neural network, i.e., the first layer consists of a convolutional layer with one input channel and <math id="S6.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="16" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>16</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">16</annotation></semantics></math> output channels, followed by batch normalization, a ReLU activation function, and max pooling; the second layer mirrors the first but has <math id="S6.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="16" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>16</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">16</annotation></semantics></math> input channels and <math id="S6.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="32" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>32</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">32</annotation></semantics></math> output channels; the last layer is a fully connected layer that predicts <math id="S6.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>10</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10</annotation></semantics></math> classes.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We conduct the experiments on a distributed system with <math id="S6.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>10</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10</annotation></semantics></math> agents and its topology is based on the Erdös-Rényi graph <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite> with the connectivity probability of <math id="S6.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=0.6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=0.6</annotation></semantics></math>.
For all algorithms, we set batch size to <math id="S6.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b=256" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>256</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b=256</annotation></semantics></math>.
The step size of D-SGD, D-SGT and DNSGD are chosen from <math id="S6.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{0.0001,0.0005,0.001,0.005,0.01,0.05,0.1,0.5,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0.0001</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.0005</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.001</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.005</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.01</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.05</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{0.0001,0.0005,0.001,0.005,0.01,0.05,0.1,0.5,1\}</annotation></semantics></math>.
For our DNSGD, we set the iterations number of Chebyshev acceleration (Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Decentralized Stochastic Nonconvex Optimization under the Relaxed Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) to <math id="S6.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{K}=K=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>K</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{K}=K=2</annotation></semantics></math>.
We show the results for the gradient norm and the test accuracy in
Figures <a href="#S6.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 6 Experiments ‣ Decentralized Stochastic Nonconvex Optimization under the Relaxed Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> and <a href="#S6.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 6 Experiments ‣ Decentralized Stochastic Nonconvex Optimization under the Relaxed Smoothness" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, demonstrating that the proposed DNSGD outperforms all baseline methods.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S6.F1" class="ltx_figure">
